<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Role in the World</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Importance of Panama in Global Trade</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Paraphrase articles and texts for clearer understanding. Communication: Aid in negotiating group roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can paraphrase articles on the Panama Canal's impact on international trade, using visuals like a map comparing shipping routes through the Canal vs. around Cape Horn (e.g., "The map shows that using the Canal saves two weeks of travel time for ships.")<br>Can help negotiate roles in group projects on global trade, using gestures and encouraging eye contact to keep focus (e.g., "Would you like to focus on the economic or environmental aspects of the Canal?")<br>Can clarify main ideas in trade-related texts for group work, using visuals like pie charts to illustrate key concepts (e.g., "Our project will cover how the Canal influences trade and local jobs.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activating prior knowledge and preparing students for the lesson's mediation focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map showing major shipping routes and highlights the Panama Canal. Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs: "How has the Panama Canal been impacting global trade over the past few decades, according to this map?" (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models paraphrasing a complex statement related to the Canal. For example: Teacher writes "The Canal's operation has necessitated significant investments in infrastructure to enhance its efficiency and prevent congestion." Teacher then paraphrases: "To keep the Canal running smoothly and avoid delays, a lot of money has been put into improving its systems." Teacher also models the pronunciation of /oʊ/ in "global" and "local" and the reduction in "environmental." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'paraphrase' mean? Does it mean copying word for word?" (No, it means expressing the same idea in your own words.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would it be useful to paraphrase something? (e.g., explaining something complicated to a friend, summarizing an article.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introducing new language and content in context, focusing on the scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Role in the World":</b> Students receive a short article (approx. 200 words) titled "The Enduring Influence of the Panama Canal on 21st-Century Shipping." The article uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>operation, traffic, congestion, routes, shipping, trade, infrastructure, efficiency, costs, investments, environmental groups, ecosystems, authority, global relationships, influence, future</i>) and grammatical structures (passive voice, present perfect continuous, second/third conditionals).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic text adapted from a news report or economic journal excerpt, medium register, informational format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student uses the English language effectively to mediate and summarize information from trade-related articles for a specific purpose.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of the article?" "What are two key challenges or successes mentioned?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students mark statements about the article as True or False, e.g., "The Canal has been causing environmental concerns for many years." "If the Canal had not been expanded, global trade would be more efficient."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focusing on accuracy through controlled and semi-controlled practice of target language and mediation skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice paraphrasing sentences, clarifying meaning, and using the passive voice, second/third conditionals, and present perfect continuous. Emphasis on vocabulary like <i>prevent, invest, enhance, raise concerns, affect, impact, influence, navigate, control, oversee, transport, adjust</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Paraphrasing:</b> Teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences from the article (or similar on the topic) containing target grammar/vocabulary. Students work individually to paraphrase each sentence in their own words, ensuring they retain the core meaning. Example: "The Canal has been managed by the Panama Canal Authority since 1999." -> Paraphrase: "The Panama Canal Authority has controlled the Canal's operations for over two decades now." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Hypothetical Scenarios (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs, students complete conditional sentences related to the Canal's influence. Teacher provides sentence beginnings: "If the government had not invested in the Canal's expansion,..." or "Global shipping costs would be much higher if..." Students complete the sentences, practicing third conditionals and vocabulary like <i>investments, costs, efficiency, delays</i>. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Clarifying Main Ideas (Pair Interaction):</b> Students receive an image (e.g., a pie chart showing types of cargo transported through the Canal or a graph showing increase in traffic). In pairs, one student describes the visual, and the other student must paraphrase the main ideas of the visual description. They use phrases like "So, what you're saying is..." or "To clarify, the main point is that..." Teacher encourages encouraging eye contact and gestures to aid understanding. (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, culminating in the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group presentation of the "Global Trade Infographic" project (developed over previous lessons) to another group. This stage heavily relies on students' ability to mediate information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in their project groups (3-4 students). Each group takes turns presenting their Global Trade Infographic on "The Importance of Panama in Global Trade" to another group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Focus:</b> During the presentation, students must:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Paraphrase:</b> Clearly explain complex information or statistics from their infographic in simpler terms for the audience. (e.g., "This section, where it states 'Global shipping routes are significantly optimized,' means ships save a lot of time and fuel by using the Canal.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarify:</b> Address any questions from the audience, ensuring understanding of key concepts, using visuals like a map comparing shipping routes. (e.g., "Would you like me to clarify how the Canal prevents global traffic congestion?")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Negotiate Roles (within presenting group):</b> Students within the presenting group verbally negotiate who explains which part of the infographic, or who handles questions, using phrases like, "Would you like to focus on the economic or environmental aspects of the Canal?" or "I can explain the passive voice sentences if you handle the conditionals." (Teacher observes negotiation process.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively use target grammar (passive voice: "The Canal is operated by...", "Investments have been made to enhance...") and vocabulary (e.g., <i>efficiency, costs, delays, global relationships, influence</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Employ respectful language (sociolinguistic competence: "The Canal supports global commerce.") and appropriate trade-related terms.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing group interactions, listening for target language use, and providing feedback on mediation strategies.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and self-reflection on learning outcomes and performance.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their Global Trade Infographic to another group, demonstrating mediation skills. Peers use a simple checklist to provide feedback on clarity and understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectively paraphrases complex information from the infographic.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifies main ideas using visuals and appropriate language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Aids in negotiating group roles during presentation (observed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target grammar (passive voice, conditionals, present perfect continuous) accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary appropriately and pronounces /oʊ/ and multisyllabic words clearly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy I used today to help my group understand information better was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easiest/most challenging to (paraphrase a text / clarify a visual / negotiate roles) today because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reviewing the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now in explaining complex information about the Panama Canal to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of mediating information (paraphrasing, clarifying, negotiating) do I want to improve next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The group presentations allowed for authentic practice of clarifying and paraphrasing. Students effectively used visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson/unit? Perhaps more explicit modeling of negotiation phrases. Some students still struggled with simplifying complex technical terms. I need to reinforce pronunciation of words like "impactful" and "environmental."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "The Importance of Panama in Global Trade." The next theme will build on communication skills by exploring another aspect of Panama's global relationships, possibly focusing on tourism or cultural exchange, using similar structures and new vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>